1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,075
Blue wants a chain from the top to the
bottom.

2
00:00:03,075 --> 00:00:06,082
Red wants a chain from the left to the
right.

3
00:00:06,082 --> 00:00:08,556
They take turns claiming empty cells.

4
00:00:08,556 --> 00:00:13,368
The question is not just who wins, but how
cheaply Blue can force a win.

5
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:16,705
Two cells connect when they share an edge.

6
00:00:16,705 --> 00:00:19,310
An interior hexagon has six neighbours.

7
00:00:19,310 --> 00:00:23,584
We use ordinary alternating play, Blue
first, with no swap rule.

8
00:00:23,584 --> 00:00:28,660
A strategy must work against every legal
reply, not just a helpful opponent.

9
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:33,449
Start with this two by two board. Blue
takes the upper-right cell.

10
00:00:33,449 --> 00:00:37,763
It touches the top, and it is adjacent to
both cells on the bottom.

11
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:41,368
Blue now has two possible ways to complete
a connection.

12
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:46,362
If Red takes the lower-left cell, Blue
takes the lower-right.

13
00:00:46,362 --> 00:00:50,458
If Red takes the lower-right, Blue takes
the lower-left.

14
00:00:50,458 --> 00:00:54,115
A reply somewhere else also leaves a
winning move.

15
00:00:54,115 --> 00:00:57,260
Red cannot occupy both threats in one turn.

16
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:01,715
Notice what Blue did not need: a path
chosen before Red moved.

17
00:01:01,715 --> 00:01:04,872
Blue chose the final route after seeing the
reply.

18
00:01:04,872 --> 00:01:09,735
That difference between a fixed route and
an adaptive strategy will matter on

19
00:01:09,735 --> 00:01:10,808
the larger board.

20
00:01:11,331 --> 00:01:17,098
Move to five by five. Any top-to-bottom
path must visit all five rows, so it

21
00:01:17,098 --> 00:01:23,016
needs at least five cells. But a geometric
path on an empty board is not yet a

22
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:25,443
path Blue can force against Red.

23
00:01:25,965 --> 00:01:31,201
There are two different costs. Count the
cells in the final connecting path,

24
00:01:31,201 --> 00:01:36,369
or count every stone Blue placed. We call
the best forced path cost lambda,

25
00:01:36,369 --> 00:01:38,919
and the best forced stone cost delta.

26
00:01:38,919 --> 00:01:40,917
On five by five, they differ.

27
00:01:41,463 --> 00:01:46,189
Here is an actual branch of the certified
five-by-five strategy.

28
00:01:46,189 --> 00:01:50,915
Blue opens in the centre. We will follow
all twenty-three moves.

29
00:01:50,915 --> 00:01:54,164
Odd move numbers are Blue, even numbers
Red.

30
00:01:54,164 --> 00:01:58,815
For this branch, Red delays the connection
as long as possible.

31
00:01:59,363 --> 00:02:01,603
The stones spread across the board.

32
00:02:01,603 --> 00:02:06,339
A stone that is not on the eventual winning
route can still matter: it may

33
00:02:06,339 --> 00:02:09,027
create another threat or answer a defence.

34
00:02:09,027 --> 00:02:13,379
Keep watching which cells remain available
as the players alternate.

35
00:02:13,897 --> 00:02:16,984
Now the last seven moves complete the
position.

36
00:02:16,984 --> 00:02:19,676
Blue first connects on move twenty-three.

37
00:02:19,676 --> 00:02:24,668
This is one legal game from the strategy,
not the universal proof by itself.

38
00:02:24,668 --> 00:02:28,609
The proof must account for Red's other
possible replies too.

39
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:34,103
Trace this winning route. Start in the
top-right cell, move down-left, then

40
00:02:34,103 --> 00:02:37,286
down, across, down, and down-left to the
bottom.

41
00:02:37,286 --> 00:02:40,601
Count the cells: one, two, three, four,
five, six.

42
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:43,386
That is the shortest Blue connection here.

43
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:46,312
But Blue has placed twelve stones.

44
00:02:46,312 --> 00:02:50,024
Six are on this shortest path, and six are
elsewhere.

45
00:02:50,024 --> 00:02:55,488
This one position makes the distinction
visible: path length six does not mean

46
00:02:55,488 --> 00:02:57,730
Blue won on the sixth Blue move.

47
00:02:58,263 --> 00:03:03,593
The full certificate proves that Blue can
always produce a path of at most six

48
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:08,718
cells. Separate Red certificates prevent a
path of at most five after every

49
00:03:08,718 --> 00:03:13,911
opening. Together, these two directions
establish lambda of five equals six.

50
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:18,961
A different pair of bounds establishes
delta of five equals seven.

51
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:24,315
Red can stop a win made with six Blue
stones, while another Blue strategy wins

52
00:03:24,315 --> 00:03:29,532
using at most seven. Our twelve-stone
illustration is not a stone-minimising

53
00:03:29,532 --> 00:03:30,150
strategy.

54
00:03:30,692 --> 00:03:34,848
Why can a seemingly convincing argument
miss the shorter path?

55
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:37,396
Suppose you first commit to one route.

56
00:03:37,396 --> 00:03:40,747
Red only needs one cell on that route to
block it.

57
00:03:40,747 --> 00:03:45,842
That proves the chosen route is vulnerable,
not that every adaptive strategy

58
00:03:45,842 --> 00:03:46,244
fails.

59
00:03:46,792 --> 00:03:51,988
Return to the tiny board. Either bottom
cell blocks one proposed route, but

60
00:03:51,988 --> 00:03:56,006
leaves the other. The winning route depends
on Red's move.

61
00:03:56,006 --> 00:04:01,201
This is the quantifier order: Blue chooses
a strategy, Red replies, and the

62
00:04:01,201 --> 00:04:02,656
route can then adapt.

63
00:04:03,192 --> 00:04:08,156
A game tree expresses that order. At a Blue
turn, one successful move is

64
00:04:08,156 --> 00:04:12,086
enough. At a Red turn, every legal reply
must be handled.

65
00:04:12,086 --> 00:04:17,257
A single successful branch is evidence for
a possibility; a strategy covers

66
00:04:17,257 --> 00:04:18,912
the whole relevant tree.

67
00:04:19,458 --> 00:04:23,764
The search writes down a proposed move at
each position it needs.

68
00:04:23,764 --> 00:04:28,865
An independent checker reads those moves
and examines the opponent's replies.

69
00:04:28,865 --> 00:04:32,642
It does not assume that the search's
shortcuts were safe.

70
00:04:32,642 --> 00:04:35,226
It checks the proposed strategy itself.

71
00:04:35,758 --> 00:04:40,471
For each position, the checker verifies
that the prescribed move is legal.

72
00:04:40,471 --> 00:04:44,866
Then it tries every empty cell as the
opponent's reply and continues.

73
00:04:44,866 --> 00:04:48,815
Terminal connections are checked on the
actual occupied cells.

74
00:04:48,815 --> 00:04:52,510
This turns a strategy table into a
replayable certificate.

75
00:04:53,058 --> 00:04:57,243
The search can also view the problem as a
family of target sets.

76
00:04:57,243 --> 00:05:02,147
On five by five, there are one hundred and
six inclusion-minimal connecting

77
00:05:02,147 --> 00:05:06,921
cell sets of size at most six. Red kills a
target by occupying any of its

78
00:05:06,921 --> 00:05:07,314
cells.

79
00:05:07,858 --> 00:05:12,304
The certified central six-cell strategy was
replayed through one hundred and

80
00:05:12,304 --> 00:05:15,872
six thousand, eight hundred and twenty-six
checked positions.

81
00:05:15,872 --> 00:05:18,505
This counter summarises that retained
replay.

82
00:05:18,505 --> 00:05:22,834
It is not a live search, and the number
counts positions where a certified

83
00:05:22,834 --> 00:05:23,770
move was tested.

84
00:05:24,291 --> 00:05:26,506
There is one rule detail to settle.

85
00:05:26,506 --> 00:05:31,252
Under the continued-play definition, a long
Blue connection does not finish

86
00:05:31,252 --> 00:05:36,061
the short-path task. Play may continue
until Blue makes a sufficiently short

87
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:40,491
path. Under the strict definition, the
first connection ends the game.

88
00:05:41,024 --> 00:05:43,793
The five-by-five value is six under both
readings.

89
00:05:43,793 --> 00:05:47,336
A separate strict certificate handles the
first-connection rule.

90
00:05:47,336 --> 00:05:51,323
So the correction is not explained away by
choosing a different stopping

91
00:05:51,323 --> 00:05:55,088
convention. The two definitions could still
differ on larger boards.

92
00:05:55,617 --> 00:05:59,431
On six by six, the stone budget is exactly
ten.

93
00:05:59,431 --> 00:06:05,274
Red can prevent a connection using nine or
fewer Blue stones after every

94
00:06:05,274 --> 00:06:10,955
opening. A certified Blue strategy, opening
at row three, column four,

95
00:06:10,955 --> 00:06:13,065
succeeds with at most ten.

96
00:06:13,584 --> 00:06:18,922
Ten Blue moves mean at most nineteen moves
altogether: Blue makes ten, and Red

97
00:06:18,922 --> 00:06:24,055
moves in the nine gaps. The lower-bound
defence shows why fewer Blue stones

98
00:06:24,055 --> 00:06:29,051
cannot be guaranteed. This is a
stone-budget statement, not a path-length

99
00:06:29,051 --> 00:06:29,736
statement.

100
00:06:30,284 --> 00:06:34,860
For path length on six by six, the
certified statement is a lower bound of

101
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:39,436
eight. Every one of the thirty-six possible
openings has a Red certificate

102
00:06:39,436 --> 00:06:42,404
stopping all Blue paths of seven cells or
fewer.

103
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:47,697
Those thirty-six seven-cell defence
certificates account for twenty-two

104
00:06:47,697 --> 00:06:52,243
million, seven hundred and eighty thousand,
and ninety-seven checked

105
00:06:52,243 --> 00:06:57,056
positions. Their completed replay records
were recovered and reconciled.

106
00:06:57,056 --> 00:07:01,535
This video does not claim that the large
searches were rerun during

107
00:07:01,535 --> 00:07:02,270
production.

108
00:07:02,817 --> 00:07:08,338
A separate exhaustive search found a Blue
strategy for a path of at most eight

109
00:07:08,338 --> 00:07:13,292
cells. But that upper bound has no
independently replayed certificate.

110
00:07:13,292 --> 00:07:18,671
We therefore keep it visibly labelled
search-only, rather than presenting an

111
00:07:18,671 --> 00:07:20,865
exact certified value of eight.

112
00:07:21,384 --> 00:07:24,876
Nor does that search settle the strict
six-by-six value.

113
00:07:24,876 --> 00:07:29,241
The retained bounds for the
first-connection reading are eight through

114
00:07:29,241 --> 00:07:29,490
ten.

115
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:34,042
On five by five both readings are resolved;
on six by six an exact strict

116
00:07:34,042 --> 00:07:35,352
value is not claimed.

117
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:40,565
Campbell introduced these measures and
conjectured the small-board values in

118
00:07:40,565 --> 00:07:45,126
two thousand and four. The certified
five-by-five path value is six rather

119
00:07:45,126 --> 00:07:49,626
than the proposed seven. It also
contradicts the seven claimed in the two

120
00:07:49,626 --> 00:07:53,448
thousand and twenty-six Casco, Pacifico and
Sanhueza preprint.

121
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:58,231
The six-by-six stone value ten contradicts
the proposed nine.

122
00:07:58,231 --> 00:08:03,666
But it agrees with an earlier nineteen-move
estimate from H Z Y's KataHex work

123
00:08:03,666 --> 00:08:09,032
in two thousand and twenty-four. That prior
estimate deserves credit; the new

124
00:08:09,032 --> 00:08:11,332
evidence is the replayable proof.

125
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,816
The verification includes corrupted
certificates that the checkers reject, as

126
00:08:16,816 --> 00:08:20,342
well as valid controls. There is no Lean
formalisation.

127
00:08:20,342 --> 00:08:25,215
Confidence is high for the certified
claims, conditional on the written game

128
00:08:25,215 --> 00:08:30,151
reduction and independent checker, and more
limited for the unreplayed search

129
00:08:30,151 --> 00:08:30,600
result.

130
00:08:31,146 --> 00:08:34,705
The lesson is threefold. A strategy can
change its route.

131
00:08:34,705 --> 00:08:37,451
A short final path can require extra
stones.

132
00:08:37,451 --> 00:08:42,320
And a search result becomes stronger
evidence when a separate checker verifies

133
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,066
the strategy against every possible
defence.

134
00:08:45,613 --> 00:08:50,246
The certified results are six for the
five-by-five path, seven for its stone

135
00:08:50,246 --> 00:08:54,940
budget, ten for the six-by-six stone
budget, and at least eight for its path.

136
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:59,330
Larger boards and the exact strict
six-by-six value remain outside these

137
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:00,061
conclusions.

138
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:05,647
The paper, strategy evidence and editable
animation accompany this video.

139
00:09:05,647 --> 00:09:10,714
The prior-work search was bounded, so
originality is stated with moderate

140
00:09:10,714 --> 00:09:15,851
confidence. The diagrams replay retained
examples and explain the checking

141
00:09:15,851 --> 00:09:18,628
logic. Narration uses a synthetic voice.
